# Normalize every text file to LF, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` then pins the working-tree
# form, so a Windows checkout does not hand LF-committed files back as CRLF and
# a later scripted edit cannot flip a whole file. This repository has paid for
# that twice: once as `chore: restore LF line endings`, and again in PR #143,
# where a test-only change carried a CRLF -> LF conversion of the whole of
# `db/fts_query.rs` and inflated a 134-line diff to over 1900.
#
# All 96 tracked .rs files were already LF when this was added, so it is a
# guard against recurrence rather than a migration.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The binary declarations below must stay AFTER the line above: gitattributes
# resolves per pattern with the last match winning, so these re-exempt the
# fixture formats from any text handling.

# Binary test fixtures (feature-45: binary document formats).
# Mark explicitly so git never attempts CRLF normalization or textual diffing
# on these formats, regardless of a contributor's core.autocrlf setting.
*.pdf binary

# PR-3 (Office formats: docx/xlsx/pptx/xls) fixtures — declared ahead of time
# so newly added fixtures are covered from the first commit that adds them.
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.xls binary

# Raster images: the tray icons have ridden on git's binary auto-detection
# since v0.9.0, and README screenshots joined them in the logo/badge PR.
# Declared for the same reason as the fixtures above — the rule should not
# depend on detection succeeding. `.svg` is deliberately absent: it is text,
# and the `eol=lf` rule at the top is what should apply to it.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
